-than-truckload services, as well as expedited transportation services. It also provides various value-added services, including container drayage, truckload brokerage, and supply chain consulting. In addition, the company operates fleet maintenance centers. As of December 31, 2025, it owned and operated 10,184 tractors, 30,824 linehaul trailers, and 14,313 pickup and delivery trailers. Old Dominion Freight Line, Inc. was founded in 1934 and is headquartered in Thomasville, North Carolina.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Kevin M. Freeman | President, CEO & Director | Age 66 | Compensation: $9.2M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 20K people. Revenue per employee of $272K ranks in the below the sector median among Industrials peers, with revenue growing 9%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $47K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $637 per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 34\u00d7 revenue per employee (elevated relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (2/10). Recent headlines include layoff or workforce-reduction announcements.","financial_summary":["Old Dominion Freight Line, Inc. is a large-cap industrials business with a $34.6B market cap on $5.5B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at -4% over the past three years and +6% annually across the full decade, and a shrinking top line is the hardest headwind any business can overcome.","Returns on capital run at 33% against a 11% cost of capital \u2014 a 23-point spread that is the clearest signature of an economic moat.","The business throws off $955M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 17 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.34\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, the balance sheet carries net cash, which is the gold standard: the business never has to make desperate decisions in a bad year.","Shareholder yield of 2.8% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and insiders hold 8.3% of the shares, and within its sector the business captures 2.2\u00d7 its fair share of profits, a structural advantage visible in the numbers."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Price sits 65% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left, and the cushion against estimate error has been bid away (confirmed by 2 other valuation measures)","sev":"red","cat":"valuation","magnitude":0.6488254573082914},{"text":"Above-average short interest at 5.5% of float","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Insiders are net sellers (100% sell ratio, net $23.3M) \u2014 the people closest to the business are reducing their personal stake","sev":"amber","cat":"insider"},{"text":"Revenue growth lags most Industrials peers \u2014 losing share inside the profit pool","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"News sentiment running negative (-0.19) across recent coverage","sev":"amber","cat":"news"},{"text":"Recent layoff news \u2014 often the market\u2019s first signal that growth is under pressure or costs are being rebased","sev":"amber","cat":"news"},{"text":"Industrials ETF trading well below its long-term trend","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Cyclical sectors like Industrials tend to underperform in slowdowns","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"High market volatility with VIX at 36, signaling elevated fear","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Tariff and trade-policy exposure \u2014 industrial exporters sit squarely in the crosshairs of protectionist policy, and retaliatory tariffs can close markets without warning.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Operating margins 16% above sector median \u2014 the quantitative trace of pricing power","Altman Z comfortably in the safe zone \u2014 a fortress balance sheet on this measure","Healthy payout ratio leaves room for both dividend g